# Line endings. `text=auto` lets git detect which files are text and store them with LF
# in the repository whatever the working tree uses, so a checkout on Windows and one on
# macOS produce the same blobs. It matters here for the shell scripts specifically --
# backend/run_dev.sh and research/remote/*.sh all start with a
# shebang, and a CRLF line ending there makes the kernel look for an interpreter whose
# name ends in a carriage return.
* text=auto

# The tracked binaries: the extension's icons, and the paper's figures and PDF. Detection
# would classify these correctly on its own; marking them is what guarantees no future
# `text=` rule can reach them, and it keeps the paper's PDF -- which is rebuilt and
# recommitted often -- out of any attempt to produce a textual diff.
*.png binary
*.pdf binary
